    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d17">

                <pb facs="0083" n="15" xml:id="pg15"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">763.72119/10428</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to President Wilson </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1916-05-03">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">May 3, 1916</hi>.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">My Dear Mr. President</hi>: Enclosed is a most
                    confidential message just received from Gerard.<anchor n="8a" xml:id="d17-f1"/>
                    I am a little puzzled as to the real purpose of introducing the subject at the
                    present time. It would look to me like an attempted diversion on Germany’s part
                    in order to create here a sympathetic feeling which would prevent radical action
                    on our part. Of course I may be in error as to the motive of the German official
                    who undoubtedly inspired this telegram but I confess to be very skeptical as to
                    the <hi rendition="#italic">bona fides</hi> of the suggestion.</p>
                <p>Faithfully yours,</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Robert Lansing</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
